00:20:13.180 --> 00:20:16.500
If you're not scared, you're not taking a chance and you're not taking a chance on what the hell are you doing?
如果你不害怕，你就是在冒险，而如果不冒险，你他妈的到底在干什么？

00:20:16.500 --> 00:20:17.339
That's the one.
就是这个。

00:20:17.339 --> 00:20:18.339
I think that's great.
我觉得这太棒了。

00:20:18.339 --> 00:20:19.220
I think that's great advice.
我觉得这是个绝佳的建议。

00:20:19.220 --> 00:20:27.536
And I have found it's true like I had an acting teacher at NYU who used to say, fear is just excitement without breath.
我发现这确实如此，就像我在纽约大学的一位表演老师过去常说的，恐惧只是没有呼吸的兴奋。

00:20:27.536 --> 00:20:30.262
You realize you're holding your breath.
你意识到自己一直在屏住呼吸。

00:20:30.262 --> 00:20:31.940
You're actually excited.
其实你很兴奋。

00:20:33.059 --> 00:20:34.670
New things are so scary.
新鲜事物总是那么让人害怕。

00:20:34.670 --> 00:20:40.774
I heard it described this way by a meditation teacher that our brains are like accountants.
我听一位冥想老师这样描述过：我们的大脑就像会计师。

00:20:40.774 --> 00:20:43.525
They can only tell you where you've been.
它们只能告诉你你去过哪里。

00:20:43.525 --> 00:20:48.019
They sift through the receipts and they tell you where you've been.
它们梳理着收据，然后告诉你你到过何方。

00:20:48.019 --> 00:20:55.740
So your brain actually wants you to repeat the things it has done, you've already done, and it's catalogued and deemed safe.
所以你的大脑其实希望你重复那些它已经做过、你已经做过、并且被它归档认定为安全的事情。

00:20:55.740 --> 00:21:01.859
But all the gold in life is in risk and newness and adventure and taking a leap, right?
但生活中的所有黄金都在于冒险、尝新、历险和奋力一跃，对吧？

00:21:01.859 --> 00:21:05.180
But your brain is always gonna say, are you sure about that?
但你的大脑总是会说，你确定吗？

00:21:05.180 --> 00:21:06.380
I don't have receipts for that.
我可没有那方面的收据。

00:21:06.380 --> 00:21:09.539
I don't know when that's like, maybe we shouldn't do it.
我不知道那算什么，也许我们不该去做。

00:21:09.539 --> 00:21:16.299
But it's, you got a leap frog, you know, pole vault or whatever over that part of your brain.
但你得跨栏，你知道的，撑竿跳或者用别的方法越过大脑的那一部分。

00:21:16.299 --> 00:21:19.900
Sometimes if your brain is like, I think that looks unsafe.
有时如果你的大脑觉得，这看起来不安全。

00:21:19.900 --> 00:21:30.380
Like maybe pay attention, like you don't want to put yourself in grave danger, but also your brain will respond as if it's grave danger when it's just like a social invitation.
就像或许该注意点，你不想让自己陷入极度危险之中，但同时，你的大脑在面对仅仅是一场社交邀请时，也会表现得好像面临弥天大祸一样。

00:21:30.380 --> 00:21:33.180
You're scared to go to the party or whatever, you know.
你害怕去参加派对什么的，你知道的。

00:21:33.180 --> 00:21:36.930
But yeah, if you're not scared, you're not taking a chance.
但没错，如果你不害怕，你就是在冒险。

00:21:36.930 --> 00:21:41.529
If you're not taking a chance of what the hell you're doing, I like that I'm gonna stick with that one.
如果你不冒险，你他妈的到底在干什么，我喜欢这个，我就选这个了。

00:21:41.529 --> 00:21:44.923
Gillie NZ underscore 83 says two part question.
Gillie NZ underscore 83 提出了一个两部分的问题。

00:21:44.923 --> 00:21:49.110
Who was the guest star that made you fangirl out the most?
哪个客串明星最让你秒变狂热粉丝？

00:21:49.110 --> 00:21:53.369
And who do you remember killed at the most as a guest star?
你觉得哪个客串明星作为嘉宾表现得最惊艳？

00:21:55.640 --> 00:22:01.559
I remember being very delighted that Martin Short was a guest star on the show.
我记得马丁·肖特（Martin Short）来剧里客串时，我感到非常高兴。

00:22:02.759 --> 00:22:07.519
And I remember, am I still coming through here, Alec?
而且我记得，艾利克，我的声音还能传过来吗？

00:22:07.519 --> 00:22:08.319
Yeah, you're good.
对，没问题。

00:22:08.319 --> 00:22:08.819
Yeah.
对。

00:22:08.819 --> 00:22:13.118
Martin Short also, I think I don't remember what season it was.
马丁·肖特也是，我想不起来是哪一季了。

00:22:13.359 --> 00:22:22.079
It was a later season, but there's real bloopers of him making me, in particular, laugh.
那是后来的某一季，有他逗得我尤其是哈哈大笑的真实NG花絮。

00:22:22.079 --> 00:22:29.400
He seemed to delight in the anarchy of, you know, getting people to break.
他似乎很享受那种混乱感，你知道的，总想让人笑场。

00:22:29.400 --> 00:22:34.660
I, yeah, I loved Martin Short.
我，是的，我很喜欢马丁·肖特。

00:22:34.660 --> 00:22:39.420
Who else was, I mean, it was really fun when Tim Gunn was there.
还有谁来着，我是说，蒂姆·古恩（Tim Gunn）来的时候真的很有趣。

00:22:39.420 --> 00:22:43.220
But also, Tim Gunn and I have the same birthday, and he knew that.
而且，蒂姆·古恩和我同一天生日，他知道这一点。

00:22:43.220 --> 00:22:45.420
he like came up to set with that fact.
他带着这个事实来到片场。

00:22:45.420 --> 00:22:48.940
He was like, July 29th, he's like me and you.
他当时说，7月29日，我和你一样。

00:22:50.740 --> 00:23:12.660
And I'm trying to think, I mean, there were a couple other, like Franny Conroy who played Barney's mom, Edward Herman, like sometimes they were just like great New York theater actors that I knew that were legends in the New York theater that maybe the people out in the world don't know as well, but they were really thrilling to me.
我在想要不要提，我是说，还有其他一些人，比如饰演巴尼妈妈的弗兰西斯·康罗伊（Franny Conroy）、爱德华·赫曼（Edward Herman），有时他们只是一些我认识的伟大的纽约戏剧演员，是纽约戏剧界的传奇人物，也许外界的人对他们不太熟悉，但他们对我来说真的超级激动人心。

00:23:13.220 --> 00:23:17.075
And then you just get to know people and you really like them.
然后你慢慢了解了这些人，你会真的很喜欢他们。

00:23:17.075 --> 00:23:18.256
I like Chris Ellie.
我喜欢克里斯·埃利奥特（Chris Elliott）。

00:23:18.256 --> 00:23:23.044
It's a great guy and You know, there were there was just so many guest stars.
他是个好人，而且，你知道的，有那么多的客串明星。

00:23:23.044 --> 00:23:34.050
It's hard to think of them all right now now Ami DWV asks a question that gives us a great moment to kind of promote our own show here who asks are you still in touch with Kobe?
现在很难把他们都想起来了，Ami DWV 问了一个问题，这给了我们一个宣传自己节目的绝佳契机，她问，你现在还和寇碧（Cobie）保持联系吗？

00:23:35.529 --> 00:23:40.442
Yes for sure, but Mostly Well, she's been on this show.
是的，当然，但主要是，好吧，她上过这个节目。

00:23:40.442 --> 00:23:42.764
She's been on the podcast.
她上过这个播客。

00:23:42.764 --> 00:23:47.230
Yeah So we got to reminisce in a really sweet way.
是的，所以我们以一种非常温馨的方式一起追忆了过去。

00:23:47.230 --> 00:23:53.470
But I also send her the occasional picture of my baby, which she loves seeing.
但我也偶尔会给她发一张我宝宝的照片，她很喜欢看。

00:23:54.670 --> 00:24:07.430
Yeah, let's make sure that everyone listening here knows we have a podcast and this is part of that podcast and still some people I know who are fans of yours, follow you on Instagram, still are new to that.
是的，我们要确保这里听节目的每个人都知道我们有一个播客，这也是该播客的一部分，而且我认识的一些喜欢你、在Instagram上关注你的粉丝，对这个节目还是新面孔。

00:24:08.470 --> 00:24:10.829
Do you want to just give a quick, the quick success?
你要不要简单介绍一下？

00:24:10.829 --> 00:24:11.710
Yeah, it's called how we made your mother.
好，节目叫《我们是如何创造你母亲的》（How We Made Your Mother）。

00:24:11.710 --> 00:24:12.710
It's me and Craig Thomas.
我和克雷格·托马斯（Craig Thomas）一起主持。

00:24:12.710 --> 00:24:15.829
Craig is a co-creator of How I Mate and Mother with Carter Bayes.
克雷格是《老爸老妈浪漫史》（How I Met Your Mother）与卡特·贝伊斯（Carter Bays）共同创剧人。

00:24:15.829 --> 00:24:27.750
Craig and I are going episode by episode, talking about the show, talking about what it was like to make it, answering all your questions about how we made the show, quite literally how we made the show.
我和克雷格一集一集地回顾这部剧，聊聊这部剧，聊聊拍摄时的感受，回答大家关于我们是如何制作这部剧的所有问题，字面意思上我们是如何制作这部剧的。

00:24:27.750 --> 00:24:45.579
And also, we're exploring from this vantage point of being 12 years after the show ended, 19 years after it premiered, why the show has had such staying power, why it continues to mint new fans every single year.
同时，我们站在节目结束12年后、开播19年后的这个视角，探讨为什么这部剧能有如此持久的生命力，为什么它每年都能吸引源源不断的新粉丝。

00:24:45.579 --> 00:24:59.519
What is it about the special mystery and magic of the show that keeps people coming back to it, quoting it, wanting it to live, and wanting to live inside of it, and how it helps people through dark times.
究竟是什么特殊的神秘感和魔力让人们不断回看它、引用它的台词、希望它继续延续、想要置身于它的世界里，以及它是如何帮助人们度过黑暗时期的。

00:24:59.519 --> 00:25:08.480
We start every episode with a voice note we get from a fan, so if you, and also we end the show with a written letter from fans.
我们在每一集的开头都会播放一条来自粉丝的语音留言，如果你们想，而且我们还会在节目结尾朗读来自粉丝的信件。

00:25:08.480 --> 00:25:18.480
So if you would like to tell us what how I made your mother means to you, please go to how we made your mother.com, H-W-M-Y-N also works, I think.com.
所以如果你想告诉我们《我们育成了你的母亲》（How I Made Your Mother）对你意味着什么，请访问 howwemedyourmother.com，HWMYN 也可以，我想.com 结尾就行。

00:25:18.480 --> 00:25:25.359
How we made your mother.com go to contact and it's pretty simple how to follow those directions.
howwemedyourmother.com，点击联系页面，按照上面的说明操作非常简单。

00:25:25.359 --> 00:25:26.559
But we'd love to hear from you.
不过我们很想听到你们的声音。

00:25:26.559 --> 00:25:30.720
It's our favorite thing about the show is to hear from you all.
了解你们大家的想法是我们做这个节目最喜欢的部分。

00:25:30.720 --> 00:25:35.720
So yeah, that's what's been going on on the podcast front.
所以，这就是播客这边的情况。

00:25:36.599 --> 00:25:45.700
And on the non-podcast front, Patrick 2-2-1-1-8-2 would like to know, did it mean a lot to you starring in Hunters considering its themes?
在非播客方面，帕特里克 2-2-1-1-8-2 想知道，考虑到《猎人》（Hunters）的主题，在其中主演对你来说意义重大吗？

00:25:47.339 --> 00:26:08.180
Yes, I mean, I loved doing it just because I've played a lot of characters like Ted who are, I would call them like the virtuous center of the show, you know, like the character through which the audience enters in, the eyes and ears for the audience.
是的，我是说，我喜欢参演，只是因为我演过很多像泰德这样的角色，我会称他们为节目的美德核心，你知道的，也就是观众借以进入剧情的角色，是观众的耳目。

00:26:08.180 --> 00:26:27.660
And I like playing those characters, and I've gotten good at playing those characters, but I always would get a little jealous of the characters who were a little more broad and a little more ridiculous, and had maybe less screen time, but came in, chewed the scenery, stole the scene, and then got the hell out.
我喜欢扮演这些角色，我也擅长扮演这些角色，但我总是会有点嫉妒那些戏路更宽、更荒诞、戏份可能少一些，但一出场就抢尽风头、大飙演技，然后麻溜滚蛋的角色。

00:26:27.660 --> 00:26:40.700
And on Hunters, I played this character Lonnie Flash, who was this incredibly arrogant, insecure, 1970s, movie star, drug addict, in and out of recovery.
而在《猎人》中，我饰演了罗尼·弗拉什（Lonnie Flash）这个角色，他是一个极其傲慢、缺乏安全感、20世纪70年代的电影明星、瘾君子，戒毒戒了又犯。

00:26:40.700 --> 00:26:46.420
And he was ridiculous and pompous and funny and ultimately heartbreaking.
他荒谬、自负、搞笑，最终又让人心碎。

00:26:46.420 --> 00:26:52.099
And I felt like I got to do more of a broad side character.
我觉得我尝试扮演了一个更多面、更宽泛的配角。

00:26:52.099 --> 00:26:53.339
But it was so much fun.
但真的太有趣了。

00:26:53.339 --> 00:26:56.259
And the cast was delightful.
而且演员阵容也很讨喜。

00:26:56.259 --> 00:26:58.539
I'm still really tight with them.
我和他们的关系至今仍然非常好。

00:26:58.539 --> 00:27:01.779
And, you know, we got to shoot in Prague.
而且，你知道的，我们去布拉格拍摄了。

00:27:01.779 --> 00:27:06.500
It felt like the closest I'll get to doing a James Bond movie or something.
感觉这是我离拍詹姆斯·邦德电影或类似作品最近的一次了。

00:27:06.500 --> 00:27:09.700
It was really, it was like international intrigue.
真的很有国际谍战的氛围。

00:27:09.700 --> 00:27:15.619
And, you know, it was about hunting Nazis and about anti-Semitism.
而且，你知道的，这是关于猎杀纳粹和反犹太主义的。

00:27:15.619 --> 00:27:20.980
And so, yeah, it was very meaningful to me, both just personally and professionally.
所以，是的，无论是在个人还是专业层面上，这对我来说都意义重大。

00:27:20.980 --> 00:27:21.819
I love that job.
我太喜欢那份工作了。

00:27:21.819 --> 00:27:24.019
And I just, I think it just went on Netflix.
而且我觉得它刚刚在Netflix上线了。

00:27:24.019 --> 00:27:26.740
So if you haven't seen Hunters, it's a great show.
所以如果你还没看过《猎人》，这是一部很棒的剧。

00:27:26.740 --> 00:27:31.480
We're just going to bounce back to music for one second.
我们马上切回音乐话题。

00:27:31.480 --> 00:27:40.299
Bianca Aleini wants to know if you have a favorite music artist right now, who you're into right now.
比安卡·阿莱尼（Bianca Aleini）想知道你现在有没有最喜欢的音乐艺术家，你最近在听谁的歌。

00:27:40.299 --> 00:27:44.720
I'm really into this instrumental pianist named Phil Cook.
我最近迷上了一位名叫菲尔·库克（Phil Cook）的器乐钢琴家。

00:27:44.720 --> 00:27:55.144
He's amazing if you want just like, like, I like to listen to a lot of instrumental music when I, when I write.
如果你只想听……就像我写作时喜欢听很多纯音乐一样，他弹得太棒了。

00:27:55.144 --> 00:27:58.056
So I don't like to have lyrics.
所以我不太喜欢有歌词的。

00:27:58.056 --> 00:28:11.579
I just discovered, I love Joni Mitchell, but I had never known her song cactus tree, which is like fast becoming my favorite Joni Mitchell song.
我最近刚发现，我很喜欢琼尼·米歇尔（Joni Mitchell），但我以前从未听过她的歌《仙人掌树》（Cactus Tree），这首歌正迅速成为我最喜欢的琼尼·米歇尔的歌。

00:28:11.579 --> 00:28:14.207
My son also really loves Van Morrison for some reason.
我儿子不知为什么也很喜欢范·莫里森（Van Morrison）。

00:28:14.207 --> 00:28:16.299
When we put on Van Morrison, he chills out.
当我们播放范·莫里森的歌时，他就会安静下来。

00:28:16.299 --> 00:28:24.299
So, I've been listening to a lot of Anne Morrison, I'm trying to, I'm looking through.
所以，我一直在听很多范·莫里森的歌，我正在试着，我正在翻看。

00:28:24.299 --> 00:28:30.069
Oh, you know it's a really good album.
哦，你知道有一张非常棒的专辑吗？

00:28:30.069 --> 00:28:40.299
This Hannah Cohen album that came out this past year is, it's got a four thing name.
汉娜·科恩（Hannah Cohen）过去这一年发行的这张专辑，它的名字有四个字。

00:28:40.299 --> 00:28:44.000
Earth Star Mountain, Hannah Cohen, great record.
《地球之星山》（Earth Star Mountain），汉娜·科恩，一张伟大的唱片。

00:28:44.000 --> 00:28:48.359
If you like Fleetwood Mac, you'll really like that.
如果你喜欢佛利伍麦克（Fleetwood Mac），你一定会喜欢这张。

00:28:48.359 --> 00:28:55.569
And the companion question from Maddick Stevens, what's your favorite book right now?
来自马迪克·史蒂文斯（Maddick Stevens）的配套问题，你现在最喜欢哪本书？

00:28:55.569 --> 00:29:00.200
I'm reading a bunch of books.
我正在读好几本书。

00:29:00.200 --> 00:29:06.730
I am reading Susan Cain's book Bittersweet, which is beautiful.
我正在读苏珊·凯恩（Susan Cain）的书《苦甜参半》（Bittersweet），非常优美。

00:29:06.730 --> 00:29:11.650
I'm reading Karen Armstrong's biography of the Prophet Muhammad.
我正在读凯伦·阿姆斯特朗（Karen Armstrong）写的《先知穆罕默德传》（Prophet Muhammad）。

00:29:11.650 --> 00:29:24.809
I know a bunch about Sufiism, but I didn't, I realized I didn't know that much about Islam And this book was recommended to me, and it's biography of the Prophet Muhammad, and I think it's wonderful.
我对苏菲主义了解不少，但我以前不知道，我意识到自己对伊斯兰教了解得其实没那么多，这本书是别人推荐给我的，它是关于先知穆罕默德的传记，我觉得它棒极了。

00:29:24.809 --> 00:29:32.769
And then I'm reading Kieran Desai's novel, The Loneliness of Sonia and Sunny, which is also very beautiful.
然后我在读基兰·德赛的小说《索尼娅和桑尼的孤独》，也非常好看。

00:29:32.769 --> 00:29:34.849
So I've got three really good books.
所以我手头有三本非常棒的书。

00:29:34.849 --> 00:29:37.009
I do that, I read a lot of books at once.
我经常这样，同时读很多书。

00:29:37.009 --> 00:29:37.849
But yeah.
不过是的。

00:29:37.849 --> 00:29:38.849
Yeah, yeah.
对，没错。

00:29:38.849 --> 00:29:52.410
This is one, it's always, I'm not sure if there's a way to answer this, because there's a bit of a, I don't know if the right word, but sometimes you don't talk about this exactly, but Kevin Aang says, have you ever regretted not accepting a role?
这是一个……总觉得，我不知道怎么回答，因为这里面有点……我不知道用这个词对不对，但有时你不会具体聊这个，不过凯文·昂（Kevin Aang）问：你有没有后悔过拒绝某个角色？

00:29:52.410 --> 00:29:54.150
Yes.
有。

00:29:54.150 --> 00:29:55.470
Okay.
好吧。

00:29:55.470 --> 00:29:56.390
Do we leave it at that?
我们就这么算了吗？

00:29:56.390 --> 00:29:57.789
Yes.
是的。

00:29:57.789 --> 00:30:01.630
I have two or three that kind of haunt me a little bit.
我有两三个角色至今让我有点念念不忘。

00:30:01.630 --> 00:30:05.589
But I will say this, I won't say what they are.
但我得说，我不会说是哪几个。

00:30:05.589 --> 00:30:11.589
Because someone else played those roles and they played them well.
因为别人演了这些角色，而且演得很好。

00:30:11.589 --> 00:30:17.430
And I don't think it helps anyone to know that they got it, that they came to me first.
我觉得让别人知道他们拿到了这个角色、而且是先找的我，对任何人都没有帮助。

