00:39:13.774 --> 00:39:16.842
Okay, we've got time for two more questions.
好了，我们还有时间回答最后两个问题。

00:39:16.842 --> 00:39:17.888
And here's one.
这是其中一个。

00:39:17.888 --> 00:39:27.441
And we've talked about it, of course, on the show, but for new listeners, Abhi ABHI 25N asks, who came up with the idea for this podcast?
我们在节目里当然也讨论过这个，但对于新听众来说，Abhi ABHI 25N问：谁想出了做这个播客的主意？

00:39:27.289 --> 00:39:34.969
I would say it was a kind of alley-oop between my wife and me and Craig.
我觉得这是我和我太太还有克里斯之间的一次默契配合。

00:39:34.969 --> 00:39:44.239
So what happened was my wife had never seen the show, which I've said before was a great help in our courtship.
事情是这样的，我太太以前从没看过这部剧，我以前说过，这对我俩的追求过程非常有帮助。

00:39:45.320 --> 00:39:52.079
Because I mean, sometimes you, if you realize people are fans of the show, you're like, who do you think you're dating right now?
因为有时候，如果你发现别人是这部剧的粉丝，你就会想：你现在觉得你在和谁约会呢？

00:39:52.079 --> 00:39:56.119
Like, you know, you want there to be a clear distinction.
就像，你知道的，你希望界限能分得清清楚楚。

00:39:56.119 --> 00:39:59.679
But she had never seen the show, which was great.
但她从没看过这部剧，这很棒。

00:39:59.679 --> 00:40:05.639
But she came to me, I don't know, a couple of summers ago and said I would really like to see the show.
不过大概在几个夏天前，她来找我，说她真的很想看这部剧。

00:40:05.639 --> 00:40:08.360
I missed this huge chapter of your life.
我错过了你生命中的一大块重要篇章。

00:40:08.360 --> 00:40:13.760
I think I'll be able to be clear about who I married to versus who I'm watching on TV.
我想我能分得清我嫁的是谁，以及我在电视上看到的是谁。

00:40:13.760 --> 00:40:16.400
And she said I'd love to watch it, so can we watch it?
她说她很想看，所以我们能一起看吗？

00:40:16.400 --> 00:40:33.760
And then I thought, well, why don't, you know, it's enough time has passed and I called Craig kind of immediately and I said, Jordana wants to start watching the show, but why don't you and I, would you want to do something a little more formal and watch the show together and do a podcast and talk about it?
然后我想，嗯，为什么不呢？你知道的，已经过去足够长的时间了。我几乎立刻就给克里斯打了电话，我说乔丹娜想开始看这部剧了，但你我为什么不——你想不想搞得稍微正式一点，一起看这部剧，然后做个播客来聊聊它？

00:40:33.760 --> 00:40:44.320
Because I also love Craig and always miss Craig and want to spend more time with Craig, but life takes over and we weren't seen as much of each other as I would have liked.
因为我也爱克里斯，总是很想念克里斯，想花更多时间陪克里斯，但生活总是被各种琐事占据，我们见面的次数没有我希望的那么多。

00:40:44.320 --> 00:40:55.119
We immediately called Alec Lev, our producer who I'm talking to right now, and I don't know, by the end of the day, we're on the line with our agents and getting it all hammered out.
我们立刻给我们的制作人亚历克·莱夫打了电话——也就是我现在正在和他说的那个人。我不知道，到那天结束时，我们已经和经纪人们通上话，把这事敲定下来了。

00:40:55.119 --> 00:40:56.000
weirdly fast.
快得有点诡异。

00:40:56.000 --> 00:40:58.167
And then I came up very quickly.
然后这个名字很快就出来了。

00:40:58.167 --> 00:41:01.418
I just said, what about how we made your mother?
我当时就说，叫《老爸老妈的浪漫史》制作过程怎么样？

00:41:01.418 --> 00:41:03.449
And that was kind of the name.
名字差不多就是这么定下来的。

00:41:03.449 --> 00:41:05.616
Like, it was all just very fast.
一切都发生得非常快。

00:41:05.616 --> 00:41:06.116
Yes.
是的。

00:41:06.116 --> 00:41:06.616
Yes.
是的。

00:41:06.616 --> 00:41:07.116
Okay.
好吧。

00:41:07.116 --> 00:41:09.554
So to, I said two more now two more.
所以，我说还有两个，现在还剩两个。

00:41:09.554 --> 00:41:12.398
This is more of a comment and it's lovely.
这更像是一个评论，而且非常可爱。

00:41:12.398 --> 00:41:19.441
And it obviously goes to what you were saying that so many people have written in saying similar things.
这显然印证了你刚才所说的，有那么多人写信来表达类似的感受。我可以透露的是，我们刚刚录制的一期节目可能还没播出，现在先不说太多细节，但这是一集非常非常特别的节目，嘉宾是一位这部剧对他意义重大的观众，所以请大家留意一个非常特别的彩蛋集，那基本就是我们和一个剧迷在聊天。并且，Amid WV说我已经问过——我不确定他们之前问了什么——但我只是想说，我目前的生活正陷入低谷。

00:41:19.441 --> 00:41:50.729
And I could say that it probably will not have aired yet that we just recorded won't say too much about it now but a very very very special episode with someone who watched the show that it meant a lot to them so so be on the lookout for a a very special bonus episode that's pretty much us talking to a fan of the show and and Amid WV says I already asked I'm not sure what they already asked but I just want to say that I'm struggling in my life at the moment.
所以我回头去重温这部剧，因为它真的能帮到我。

00:41:50.729 --> 00:41:54.792
So I went back to watch the show because it really helps me.
我只想说非常感谢你，乔什。

00:41:54.792 --> 00:41:57.704
I just want to say thank you so much, Josh.
我真的很感激你。

00:41:57.704 --> 00:41:59.329
I really appreciate you.
哦，是的。

00:41:58.710 --> 00:41:59.246
Oh yeah.
哇。

00:41:59.246 --> 00:41:59.746
Wow.
这太棒了。

00:41:59.746 --> 00:42:00.885
That's wonderful.
我很高兴你现在情况好转了。

00:42:00.885 --> 00:42:03.164
I'm glad you're in a better place.
而且，你知道的，我前几天和杰西·泰勒·弗格森（曾出演《摩登家庭》）以及雷恩·威尔逊（《办公室》里的雷恩）有过这样的交流。

00:42:03.164 --> 00:42:13.352
And I, you know, I've had these conversations with Jesse Tyler Ferguson, who was on Modern Family and RAINN the other day, RAINN Wilson from the office.
我们都一致认为，最特别的事情就是听到人们说：“我当时正经历一段非常艰难的时期，而你们的节目减轻了我的负担，感觉就像在黑暗时刻有一只手在握着我。”

00:42:13.352 --> 00:42:29.571
And we both, we all agreed that the most special thing is to hear from people who said I was going through a really hard time, and your show lightened my burden, your show just kind of felt like it was holding my hand through the dark moment.
当你拍摄的时候，你根本不会意识到你的节目对某个人来说竟然有这种意义。

00:42:29.340 --> 00:42:35.380
And you don't realize that your show might be that for someone when you're filming it.
但我很高兴能成为能够带给你这种力量的事物的一部分。

00:42:35.380 --> 00:42:37.659
But I'm so glad to have been a part of something that can be that for you.
所以谢谢你让我知道这一切。

00:42:37.659 --> 00:42:40.010
So thanks for letting me know.
最后，连续下划线ephemera问：扮演一个把生活讲成精心编排的故事的人……

00:42:40.010 --> 00:42:48.570
And finally, continuous underscore ephemera asks, playing a man who tells his life as a carefully curated story.
……这是否会改变你对自己记忆的看法？究竟是我们书写了自己的人生，还是仅仅作为旁白试图在混乱中寻找逻辑？

00:42:48.570 --> 00:42:57.530
to change how you think about your own memories, whether we're authors of our lives, or just narrators trying to make chaos coherent.
天哪。

00:42:57.530 --> 00:42:59.130
Geez.
我知道，我总是抛给你……

00:42:59.130 --> 00:43:00.130
I know, I always give you.
这是最后一个，我把这最后一个留到现在。

00:43:00.130 --> 00:43:01.690
It's the last one, I hold on to that last one.
对，对，这是一个很好的问题。

00:43:01.690 --> 00:43:03.889
Yeah, yeah, that's a great question.
你知道的，我写日记有时断有时续。

00:43:05.400 --> 00:43:08.880
You know, I go in and out of journaling.
我前几天其实刚跟我太太提起这个，因为我儿子，那天早上我们和他在一起。

00:43:08.880 --> 00:43:21.429
I actually said this to my wife the other day because my son, we were with him in the morning And I realized there was something that he used to do, that he's no longer doing.
我突然意识到他以前有个习惯，现在不做了。

00:43:21.429 --> 00:43:23.510
I can't even remember what it was.
我甚至记不起那是什么习惯了。

00:43:23.510 --> 00:43:27.550
But I said to her, I wish I had journaled more.
但我对她说，我真希望自己以前能多写写日记。

00:43:27.550 --> 00:43:29.110
Like, I was too tired.
就好像，我当时太累了。

00:43:29.110 --> 00:43:42.150
But I wish I had really journaled more so that we would just have more of a beyond the many, many pictures we've taken, just have more of a log of all these changes and document them.
但我真希望自己能多记点日记，这样除了拍过的许许多多照片之外，我们还能有更多关于这些变化的记录，把它们存档。

00:43:43.349 --> 00:44:22.739
And I have a, I suppose a complicated relationship nostalgia because while I want to kind of catalog or look back at these different chapters of my life and look back with hopefully some fondness or realize the lessons I learned, there's also, you know, I'm a meditator and attempting to live a spiritual life which necessitates being in the present and not being so obsessed with the past and just being with what's right in front of me, which is this right now is the only thing that's happening.
而且我对于怀旧，我想有着一种复杂的感情。一方面，我很想把这些不同的人生篇章归档或回望，希望带着某种喜爱去回顾，或者认识到我所学到的教训；但另一方面，你知道的，我是一个冥想者，我正试图过一种精神层面的生活，这要求我们必须活在当下，不能过度执着于过去，只需和眼前的事物在一起——此时此刻，这就是唯一正在发生的事。

00:44:22.739 --> 00:44:29.219
So I think there are pleasures to be had in looking backwards and there's also dangers to be had in looking backwards.
所以我觉得，回望过去有回望过去的乐趣，但回望过去也有回望过去的危险。

00:44:29.219 --> 00:44:52.920
I think one of the things I'm writing a piece, co-writing a screenplay that's a lot about memory and the fallibility of memory and how I met your mother deals with that too where So Craig and I, we've talked about the podcast, like, is this, is what you're seeing, Ted, the narrator's version of it, or are we seeing the actual thing?
我想其中一件事是，我正在写一部作品，和别人合写一个电影剧本，里面有很多关于记忆、记忆不可靠的内容，正如《老爸老妈的浪漫史》也在探讨的那样——所以克里斯和我，我们一直在讨论这个播客，比如：你现在看到的这些，是叙述者泰德眼中的版本，还是我们看到的真实情况？

00:44:52.920 --> 00:44:53.920
Like it's very confusing.
这真的很让人困惑。

00:44:53.920 --> 00:44:54.920
I don't know.
我不知道。

00:44:54.920 --> 00:44:59.079
That's for the chat room to figure out.
那就留给聊天室的观众去琢磨吧。

00:44:59.079 --> 00:45:09.230
But I think that, you know, it's up to us to find meaning in what I think you said chaos.
但我认为，你知道的，我们要负责在——我想你刚才用了“混乱”这个词——中找到意义。

00:45:09.230 --> 00:45:11.710
I don't actually believe it's chaos.
我其实不认为那是混乱。

00:45:11.710 --> 00:45:12.989
I don't believe it's chaos.
我不相信那是混乱。

00:45:12.989 --> 00:45:18.110
I believe that until we find meaning in it, it can present as chaos.
我相信，在我们为之赋予意义之前，它可能会呈现为混乱。

00:45:18.110 --> 00:45:38.389
But once we find the meaning in it, and once we get the lesson, and once we have the TED-like 20, 25-year lens on things, I think a lot of people have had the experience of being incredibly lost or having a chapter that felt really dark and really hard.
但一旦我们在其中找到了意义，一旦我们吸取了教训，一旦我们拥有了像泰德那样历经20、25年的视角来看待事物，我想很多人都有过这样的经历：曾感到无比迷茫，或者经历过一段感觉极其黑暗、极其艰难的人生阶段。

00:45:38.389 --> 00:45:58.369
And then the clouds part and something happens and you get to a place where you realize that your character was forged in that moment or you found a new kind of grit or resilience and you never would be at the quite wonderful place that life took you had you not journeyed through that dark wood.
然后乌云散开，某些事情发生了，你来到了这样一个人生阶段：你意识到你的性格就是在那个时刻被塑造的，或者你发现了一种全新的坚韧与弹性，而如果你没有走过那片黑暗的森林，人生绝不会把你带到如今这个如此美妙的地方。

00:45:58.369 --> 00:46:00.849
There's something about our brain, this is what we've talking about about a brain.
我们的脑子里似乎有某种机制——这就是我们一直在讨论的大脑的某种特质。

00:46:00.849 --> 00:46:09.010
There's something about our brains that want our lives to be struggle-free and that we just want to go from triumph to triumph.
我们的脑子里有一种倾向，希望生活是毫无挣扎的，只想从一个胜利走向另一个胜利。

00:46:09.010 --> 00:46:14.010
And it's not, it yields nothing, like it's ridiculous.
但这根本行不通，毫无结果，简直荒谬。

00:46:14.070 --> 00:46:20.570
If we did that, we would be quite shallow creatures and we would stop succeeding anyway because we would have nothing to talk about.
如果我们真那样做了，我们就会成为非常浅薄的生物，而且无论如何我们最终都会停止成功，因为我们无话可说了。

00:46:21.570 --> 00:46:25.730
All this stuff is in the dirt, you know?
所有这些真正的干货都在泥土里，你知道吗？

00:46:25.730 --> 00:46:33.769
It's when we, you know, I just read a thing about success and the shadow and hubris and all this stuff I was reading this morning.
就在我们——我今天早上刚读到一篇关于成功、阴暗面、傲慢以及诸如此类事情的文章。

00:46:33.769 --> 00:46:42.250
And it said, you know, keep your eye on the next mountain you're going to climb, but make sure when you're in the valley, you learn the lessons of the valley.
上面说，盯紧你要攀登的下一座山，但确保身处山谷时，要汲取山谷里的教训。

00:46:42.250 --> 00:47:00.329
It's interesting that you have forged a re-watch podcast that I think actually very much goes along with what you're saying here that this was never going to be from that first conversation, the one and only one that we needed to have to decide like you're going to do this.
很有意思的是，你打造了一个重温节目的播客，我觉得这实际上非常符合你在这里所说的话：从最初的对话开始，这就从来不是那种我们只需要进行一次、然后就能拍板决定“你必须做这个”的普通谈话。

00:47:00.329 --> 00:47:07.377
The first conversation, you knew you weren't just going to talk about each scene that happened in the show.
在最初的对话中，你就知道你不会仅仅去聊剧中发生的每一个场景。

00:47:07.377 --> 00:47:09.221
And I remember filming this.
我记得当时拍摄的情景。

00:47:09.221 --> 00:47:28.979
You knew that you were going to connect it to being the person you are now, the age you are now, the actor, artist, father, husband that you are now, because there's looking back gives you, I guess, the purpose of looking back right is to give you some sort of perspective on where you are right now.
你知道你会把剧中的经历与现在的自己、现在的年龄，以及你现在作为演员、艺术家、父亲、丈夫的身份联系起来，因为回望过去赋予了你——我想回望过去的意义，不正是为了让你对当下的自己有一个某种程度的视角吗？

00:47:28.979 --> 00:47:37.409
And so I think you have forged a rewatch podcast very much in line with that spiritual version of looking back at being present.
所以我觉得，你打造了一个非常符合那种精神内核的重温播客，既回望过去，又立足当下。

00:47:37.409 --> 00:47:54.210
Yeah, I, you know, if you are interested in scene by scene breakdowns and talking about how certain jokes came up within the writer's room and why we chose that over that and the origin of the ducky tie, like that's all in this podcast.
是的，你知道的，如果你对逐集拆解、探讨某个笑话是如何在编剧室里诞生的、为什么我们选了这个而不是那个、以及“蓝色小号”或鸭子领带的起源感兴趣——所有这些都在这个播客里。

00:47:54.210 --> 00:47:55.690
It's not all philosophy.
它并不全是阳春白雪的哲学。

00:47:55.690 --> 00:48:05.210
And Craig and I really do like talking about who we are, who we were then, how the show changed us.
我和克里斯确实很喜欢谈论我们是谁、我们当时是谁、这部剧是如何改变我们的。

00:48:05.210 --> 00:48:12.489
So we really do wade into these deeper waters and I think that's part of what the joy for a lot of people is for the show.
所以我们确实会涉足这些更深的水域，而我认为这正是许多人看这部剧的乐趣所在。

00:48:12.489 --> 00:48:21.769
I mean, this is really like, it's like a, it's like a, if you want to get a PhD and how I met your mother, you should listen to how we made your mother.
我的意思是，这真的就像是，如果你想拿一个《老爸老妈浪漫史》的博士学位，你就应该听听《我们是如何制作老爸老妈浪漫史的》。

00:48:21.769 --> 00:48:23.730
So thank you all for joining us here.
所以谢谢大家今天加入我们。

00:48:23.730 --> 00:48:28.126
sure to follow, subscribe to our podcast, How We Made Your Mother.
